+# Runbook: the queue has stopped finishing work
+
+The page a responder reads when jobs are arriving and not finishing. It is written as the procedure
+it is, and every stage of it names four things: **the command** the responder types, **the signal**
+that says the command answered well, **the refusal** that says it did not, and **the rollback** that
+gets the queue back to where it was. The drill in [`../tests/support/drill.rs`](../tests/support/drill.rs)
+walks these stages in order against a queue it breaks on purpose, and
+`cargo test -p jobq --test spec_runbook` is the command that runs it: a stage that is not here is an
+act nobody takes.
+
+This is the skeleton. The sections are the ones an incident page needs, and the stages below are the
+ones this track's own pages have been describing all along — reading the queue, finding the rows,
+looking at the dead letters, handing back what a daemon holds, and reading the numbers again. Every
+part of every stage is `TODO` until it is written, and the drill will name the acts that are still
+missing from the procedure once the stages that are here can be walked.
+
+## Symptoms
+
+What does a responder see first, from the outside? Name the alert that would page somebody
+([`alerts.md`](alerts.md)) and what a person downstream notices before the queue does anything.
+
+## Signals
+
+| what to look at | where |
+| --- | --- |
+| `jobq_running` | `jobqc usage`, or the `signals` line of a run |
+| `jobq_claimed_total` and `jobq_succeeded_total` | the same two places |
+| `jobq_reclaimed_total` | the same two places |
+| `jobq_submitted_total` | the same two places |
+| TODO — the lines in the daemon's log that say what an attempt did | where |
+
+## Steps
+
+### 1. Read the queue before you touch it
+
+- command: TODO
+- signal: TODO
+- refusal: TODO
+- rollback: TODO
+
+### 2. Find the rows the failure is holding
+
+- command: TODO
+- signal: TODO
+- refusal: TODO
+- rollback: TODO
+
+### 3. Look at the dead letters before you act on them
+
+- command: TODO
+- signal: TODO
+- refusal: TODO
+- rollback: TODO
+
+### 4. Hand back what a daemon is holding
+
+- command: TODO
+- signal: TODO
+- refusal: TODO
+- rollback: TODO
+
+### 5. Read the numbers again, and the record of what you did
+
+- command: TODO
+- signal: TODO
+- refusal: TODO
+- rollback: TODO
+
+## Verification
+
+What does "recovered" mean, as numbers, and which command replays it? The drill a responder runs is
+`cargo test -p jobq --test spec_runbook`, and the promise it checks is the one the last stage makes.
+
+## Escalation
+
+Who do you wake up, with which numbers? And what is the one thing **not** to do, with the reason?
